USAGE -- what each program says when asked for its help==========================================================Every line below came out of the program named above it, run atthe shell with the command shown.  `-?' is the OS-9 conventionand is not universal; where a program wants another flag, thatis the one shown.  A program listed with no command prints nohelp of its own, and the line after its name says what to doinstead.  Nothing here was composed.  a2p   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- it takes an awk program file, or reads one from standard input, and writes the perl script to standard output; a switch it does not know, -h among them, is answered `Unrecognized switch'  about   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- it takes a program name and nothing else  accordian   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- d deals one, D the rest, f finds a move, u undoes, x exits  adduser   (CMDS/UUCP)      $ adduser -?       ADDUSER: add a user to or remove a user from the system       Usage: adduser [opts] [<username> [<userid>] ]       opts: -r <username>  - remove <username> from the system             -s  - allow the creation of super users             -?             - this message             acceptable command lines are:                adduser                adduser <username>                adduser <username> <userid>                adduser -r <username>                adduser -s <username>      v2.1 (94Sep30) This is free software released under the GNU General Public      License.  Please send suggestions/bug reports to:  bob@kc2wz.bubble.org  adlcomp   (CMDS/ADL)      $ adlcomp -?      Usage: /dd/CMDS/ADL/adlcomp Infile (with any of the following)      	-o Outfile		File for output      	-i Dir			Directory for INCLUDEs      	-t Dir			Directory for temp files      	-d			Output debugging info      	-w			Suppress warnings      	-e N			Max # of errors      	-g N			Max # of globals      	-m N			Max # of strings      	-n N			Max # of objects      	-p N			Max # of prep phrases      	-r N			Max # of routines      	-s N			Max # of verb phrases      	-v N			Max # of verbs  adldebug   (CMDS/ADL)      no help of its own -- it takes the compiled world and nothing else; `?' at its prompt lists the commands  adlrun   (CMDS/ADL)      $ adlrun -?      Usage: adlrun [filename] [-d] [-h] [-r savefile]  adltouch   (CMDS/ADL)      $ adltouch -?      Usage: adltouch <file> <number>  advcom   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes the source name and nothing else; a flag is read as a file  advent   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes no options; it asks its own questions  advint   (CMDS/GAMES)      $ advint -?      ADVINT v1.2 - Copyright (c) 1986, by David Betz      usage: advint [-r<rows>] [-c<columns>] [-l<log-file>] <file>  afm2tfm   (CMDS/TEXCMDS)      $ afm2tfm      afm2tfm 7.0, Copyright 1990-92 by Radical Eye Software      Usage: afm2tfm foo[.afm] [-O] [-v|-V bar[.vpl]]                       [-p|-t|-T encodingfile] [foo[.tfm]]  ag   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- there is no help switch; an unknown flag is only named back, and its options are -d, -a, -v, -V, -w, -W, -s and -o  agrep   (CMDS)      $ agrep      usage: agrep [-#cdehiklnpstvwxBDGIS] [-f patternfile] pattern [files]      summary of frequently used options:      -#: find matches with at most # errors      -c: output the number of matched records      -d: define record delimiter      -h: do not output file names      -i: case-insensitive search, e.g., 'a' = 'A'      -l: output the names of files that contain a match      -n: output record prefixed by record number      -v: output those records containing no matches      -w: pattern has to match as a word, e.g., 'win' will not match 'wind'      -B: best match mode. find the closest matches to the pattern      -G: output the files that contain a match  almanac   (CMDS)      $ almanac      Syntax of command:          "almanac [<longitude> <latitude>] <date> <time> [<timezone>]"      where          "<longitude>" is local longitude (DDD.MM,+W,-E)          "<latitude>" is local latitude (DD.MM,+N,-S)          "<date>" is calendar date (MMDD.YYYY)          "<time>" is time (24 hour format,HH.MMSS)          "<timezone>" is one of the following: "EST", "CST", "MST", or "PST"      Azimuth and altitude is not computed if the parameter "<longitude>" and      "<latitude>" was omitted.  Universal time is assumed unless a "<timezone>"      flag was supplied.  For the summer months, enter your time minus 1 hour if      daylight saving time is in effect in your local time zone.      All displayed angles are in degrees, minutes, seconds (if displayed) format      except for right ascension which is in hours, minutes, seconds format.      Azimuth is measured clockwise from north in the plane of the horizon.  alps   (CMDS)      $ alps -?      Syntax: alps [<opts>] >/<device>      Function: generate Alps ASP-1000 printer codes      Options:           -lq        letter quality mode           -norm      normal mode  anagram   (CMDS)      $ anagram      usage: anagram [ -d <dictionary> ] [ -m <number> ] [ -l ] [ -r ] string1 [ string2 . . . ]  animal   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes the file it learns from and no options  answer   (CMDS/ELM)      no help of its own -- it takes no options: run it and it asks `Message to:'  ape   (CMDS)      $ ape -?      syntax: ape {opts}      Function: write gibberish in the style of <stdin> to <stdout>      Options:      	-b[=]<num>[k]	maximum amount of source text to read (default 8k)      	-l[=]<num>		length of pattern required to match (default 4)  apfel   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes no options; it calls the Graph trap library, so load graph first  aprocs   (CMDS)      $ aprocs -?      Syntax: aprocs [<opts>]      Function: Another process utility; show processes in various formats      Options:       -a         Show only processes with CPU-Time more than 0.1 percent       -c[=]<dif> Number of columns in process tree from parent to child (def: 2)       -h[elp]    Show informations about aprocs' output       -m[=<sec>] Measure process activity between two points in time (def: 3 secs)       -n         Don't show processes in tree format (parent -> child)       -q         Fill table entries with zeroes (allows output sorting with 'qsort')       -v         Show some more process informations       -vv        Be very verbose (includes -v)       -V         Show informations about aprocs      aprocs: V1.10, (c)1996 by EKF Elektronik GmbH, 59065 Hamm, Germany  ar   (CMDS)      $ ar -?      Ar V1.2 - archive file manager      Usage:  Ar -<cmd>[<modifier>] [file .. ]            <cmd> is one of the following:               t  show table of contents for archive               u  update/add file(s) to the archive               p  print file(s) from the archive               x  extract file(s) from the archive            <modifier> is one of the following:               a  all versions (for extract)               s  supress file compression               z  read names for <cmd> from std in            File names can include the meta chars * and ?, or path lists.  ar2   (CMDS)      $ ar2 -?      Ar V2.00 - archive file manager      Usage:  Ar -<cmd>[<modifier>] archive [file .. ]            <cmd> is one of the following:               d   delete file(s) from the archive               m   move file(s) to archive (add and delete)               p   print file(s) from the archive               t   show table of contents for archive               u   update/add file(s) to the archive               x   extract file(s) from the archive            <modifier> is one of the following:               a   all versions (for extract)               bnn set max bits to 'nn' (12 default)               d   incrment debug level               o   default to 'old' archives               s   once, suppress binary; twice, suppress all file compression               z   read names for <cmd> from std in            File names can include the meta chars * and ?, or path lists.  arc   (CMDS)      $ arc -?      ARC - archive utility, Version 5.21, created on 04/22/87 at 15:05:21      Usage: arc {amufdxerplvtc}[biswnoq][g<password>] <archive> [<filename> . . .]      Where:   a   = add files to archive               m   = move files to archive               u   = update files in archive               f   = freshen files in archive               d   = delete files from archive               x,e = extract files from archive               r   = run files from archive               p   = copy files from archive to standard output               l   = list files in archive               v   = verbose listing of files in archive               t   = test archive integrity               c   = convert entry to new packing method               b   = retain backup copy of archive               s   = suppress compression (store only)               w   = suppress warning messages               n   = suppress notes and comments               o   = overwrite existing files when extracting               q   = squash instead of crunching               g   = Encrypt/decrypt archive entry      adapted to OS-9/68k by Stephan Paschedag  areacode   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- it takes area codes and nothing else; one it does not know it calls no area code  arepdaemon   (CMDS/ELM)      no help of its own -- it takes no options: it reads USR/LIB/ELM/autoreply.data and runs as a daemon  argproc_demo   (CMDS)      $ argproc_demo -help      argproc_demo: Usage:  file ...      Demonstration program for argproc().  Note that no spaces are allowed      between a switch and its arguments (i.e. -x3 is okay, -x 3 is not allowed).      Options:          -help    Print this m