g or piping.      	FILE	read input from one or more FILEs instead of stdin.      	-i	output the Interactive commands.      	-h	output this Help list.      	-	the null option can be used to avoid an empty command tail.      Examples:      	fortune -l | crypto -r      	crypto -eu puzzles      	crypto -n -gzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba code.file >result.file  csl   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- Microware's C standard library trap handler, not a program  csl020   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- Microware's C standard library trap handler built for the 68020, not a program  ctags   (CMDS)      $ ctags -?      ctags: illegal option -- ?      usage: ctags [-BFadtuwvx] [-f tagsfile] file ...              -B : use backward searching patterns (? ?)              -F : use forward searching patterns (/ /)              -a : append to 'tags' file              -d : create tags for #define's without arguments              -f : place tag description in 'tagsfile'              -t : create tags for typedefs, structs, unions              -u : update specified files in 'tags'              -v : prepare index for 'vgrind'              -w : suppress warnings              -x : prepare xref index  ctags.elvis   (CMDS/REBUILT)      $ ctags.elvis -?      usage: ctags [flags] filenames...      	-s  include static functions      	-t  include typedefs      	-v  include variable declarations      	-r  generate a "refs" file, too      	-a  append to "tags", instead of overwriting  cursive   (CMDS)      $ cursive -?      usage: cursive [-tn] [-in] message  cut   (CMDS)      $ cut -?      cut: Illegal option -- ?      cut [-d<delimitor>] [-s] -c<list>|-f<list> [files]  cuts   (CMDS)      $ cuts -h                CUTS Usage Summary      To decode one or more CUTS-encoded files:           cuts -d <file> . . .      To encode one or more files into a CUTS package:           cuts -e [-o <output_file>]  [ [-<type>] <file> ]         where <output