mmaries for each mailbox or folder.      -S	summarize the number of messages in each mailbox or folder.      -s status only select messages with the specified status.      	'status' is one of "new", "old", "unread" (same as "old"),      	or "read".  The first letter need only be specified.      -v	print a verbose header.  fstat   (CMDS)      $ fstat -?      Syntax: FStat [<opts>] <file1> [<opts>]      Function: Display file descriptor information      Options:          -s  display file segment list  fstopgm   (CMDS/NETPBM)      no help of its own -- a filter with no options: an image on standard input or a file name, the result on standard output  fudd   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- pipe English text through it; r and l become w, th becomes d  fuddle   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes no options and asks whether you want white  funzip   (CMDS)      no help of its own -- a filter with no options: a zip archive on standard input, its first member out  g   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes no options; it calls the Graph trap library, so load graph first  g3topbm   (CMDS/NETPBM)      $ g3topbm -?      usage:  g3topbm [-kludge][-reversebits][-stretch] [g3file]  game   (CMDS/GAMES)      no help of its own -- it takes the game listing and the page numbers and nothing else  gawk   (CMDS)      $ gawk -?      Syntax   : gawk [<opts>] <program-file> [<file>]      Function : pattern scanning and processing language      Options  :         -a             use traditional syntax for regular expressions         -e             use egrep syntax for regular expressions         -f <file>      read program source from <file>         -F <fs>        use <fs> for input field separator         -v <var>=<val> assign <val> to variable <var>         --             signal end of options        Adapted to OS-9/68k by Stephan Paschedag  gcc   (CMDS/GCC139)      $ load /dd/CMDS/cio; chx /dd/CMDS/GCC2; gcc -?    